        "heading": "About Ume",
        "heading_size": "h2",
        "intro": "<p>Ume is a small, fragrant Japanese fruit—often called a Japanese plum—known for its intensely tart flavor. Because fresh ume is naturally very sour, it has long been preserved with salt. This traditional preparation is called <em>umezuke</em>; when the fruit is later sun-dried, it becomes <em>umeboshi</em>.</p><p>Ume has been part of Japanese life for well over a thousand years. Introduced from China and embraced in Japan by the Nara period, it was originally valued as much for its practical and restorative qualities as for its flavor. Salt-preserved ume accompanied rice as a natural preservative, while other preparations were traditionally enjoyed to awaken the appetite, support digestion, and restore the body during periods of heat, illness, or fatigue.</p><p>Naturally rich in citric and other organic acids, ume also contains antioxidant plant compounds known as polyphenols. Together, these compounds may help support the body’s natural defenses, healthy digestion, and everyday energy metabolism. For generations, ume has been turned to as a small but powerful food for immunity, recovery, and renewed vitality.</p><p>A little goes a long way—bringing brightness, depth, and centuries of Japanese wisdom to the everyday table.</p>",
